+::a43a1 They solve two different problems, and neither replaces the other.
+
+::a43a2 The fingerprint answers the question _whether something has changed_. ::a43a3 It is computed from the content of a commit and from the fingerprint of the preceding one. ::a43a4 If an old contribution is altered afterwards, every fingerprint from that point on changes, and the chain no longer matches the one you saw before. ::a43a5 A change thus becomes reliably visible. ::a43a6 Which of the two versions is the published one, however, the fingerprint does not say.
+
+::a43a7 The signature answers the question _who vouches for it_. ::a43a8 With it the platform testifies that this very version is the one it published. ::a43a9 Without it a fingerprint you noted down is merely your own claim, which the platform can contradict. ::a43a10 With it you hold a statement of the platform's own, and it cannot take that back.
+
+::a43a11 Only together do the two amount to evidence: ::a43a12 the fingerprint makes a change visible, the signature makes it attributable. ::a43a13 The contribution on provable integrity spells this out in more detail.
